    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "NSAIDs: start 24–48 h b4 menses and cont until 1–2 days after menses begin; ⍉NSAID = clearly better than another, and COX-2 inhibitors are ⍉more effective"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Estrogen/progestin contraceptive: estrogen prevents ovulation and progestin thins the endometrium → less arachidonic acid for prostaglandin synth → ↓ menstrual blood flow and contractions"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Progestin options (also for 2/2 dysmenorrhea ← endometriosis): progestin-only pills, oral dienogest, levonorgestrel IUD, etonogestrel implant, depot medroxyprogesterone acetate"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "GnRH agonists decrease dysmenorrhea but may cause low-estrogen side effects; danazol = an option but rarely used (androgenic SE)"
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
